    "['ipv6 enable', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ipv6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset protocols ipv6\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper devices, the `ipv6 enable` command from Cisco is not directly equivalent since Juniper devices enable IPv6 by default on interfaces when the IPv6 address is configured. However, if you specifically need to ensure IPv6 is enabled at the global level (similar to Cisco's `ipv6 unicast-routing`), the Juniper equivalent would be `set protocols ipv6` which enables IPv6 routing globally. \\n\\nNote: For interface-specific IPv6 configuration in Juniper, you would simply configure the IPv6 address (e.g., `set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64`), which implicitly enables IPv6 on that interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols ipv6'}]]": 1,
    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64\\n##\\n\\nThe command:\\n1. Uses Juniper's interface naming convention (ge-1/0/0 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/0)\\n2. Specifies unit 0 (Juniper's logical interface concept)\\n3. Configures the IPv6 address under the inet6 address family\\n4. Maintains the same IPv6 address and prefix length (2001:db8:1::1/64)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:1::1/64'}]]": 1,
